    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-102797",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function",
      "problem": "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6y=0$ for $x>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-6u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{5}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+6u{x}^{3}-6u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{5}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nx{u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{x}^{2}{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0"
