    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-102791",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function",
      "problem": "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{2x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{2x}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+4u{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{2x}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+4u{e}^{2x})-4({u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x})+4u{e}^{2x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}({u}^{″}+4{u}^{′}+4u-4{u}^{′}-8u+4u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{2x}\\ne 0,$  we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$",
