      "math_academy_id": "q-64989",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function",
      "problem": "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{2t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$",
      "worked_solution": "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{2t}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+2u{e}^{2t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{2t}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{2t}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t})+({u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+2u{e}^{2t})-6u{e}^{2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{2t}+5{u}^{′}{e}^{2t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}({u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$",
