    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-64958",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order",
      "problem": "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}-3y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation.",
      "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-3u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-{u}^{′}x-3u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-4$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}"
            },
