    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65020",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order",
      "problem": "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+4y=0$ for $t>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation.",
      "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t)\\cdot {t}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-3t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+4u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u-3{u}^{′}t-6u+4u) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+{u}^{′}t) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}t+{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}t+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}t+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$",
